====================================================================================================
[3] EXACT FAMILIES: does the two-point test have any discriminating power?
====================================================================================================
cylinder C_L(tau)=sum_n w_n e^{-m_n tau};  test R(t)=c(2t)/c(t)^2 >= 1 .

(3a) HN1 plateau family  nu_L = 1/2 delta_{1/L^2} + 1/2 delta_4   (HN1-H1.md:228-232)
     P is FALSE here (G_c -> 1/2 e^{-1} = 0.1839 , HZ1 0.6 / log [4]).
     L       c(L/2)         c(L)   R(L/2)=c(L)/c(L/2)^2    verdict
     8  3.03433e-01  1.83940e-01                1.99779       PASS
    64  3.03265e-01  1.83940e-01                2.00000       PASS
   512  3.03265e-01  1.83940e-01                2.00000       PASS
  4096  3.03265e-01  1.83940e-01                2.00000       PASS
 65536  3.03265e-01  1.83940e-01                2.00000       PASS
  => violations in 5 windows: 0   (P is FALSE, test passes => test cannot see P)

(3b) spread family  nu_L = sum_{j<=L^3} (1/L^3) delta_{(c/L^2)(1+j/L^3)},  c=1
     P is FALSE here (G_c -> 1 , HZ1 0.2 / log [3]).
     L       c(L/2)         c(L)         R(L/2)    verdict
     8  5.44490e-01  2.97525e-01        1.00356       PASS
    16  5.44587e-01  2.97631e-01        1.00356       PASS
    32  5.44599e-01  2.97644e-01        1.00356       PASS
    64  5.44600e-01  2.97646e-01        1.00356       PASS
  => violations: 0   (P is FALSE, test passes)

(3c) P-TRUE family  nu_L = delta_{1/sqrt L}   (m_L L = sqrt L -> infinity)
  L=8     m_L=0.3536  c(L/2)=0.243117  c(L)=5.910575e-02  R(L/2)=1.00000  PASS
  L=64    m_L=0.1250  c(L/2)=0.018316  c(L)=3.354626e-04  R(L/2)=1.00000  PASS
  L=512   m_L=0.0442  c(L/2)=0.000012  c(L)=1.489490e-10  R(L/2)=1.00000  PASS

(3d) free massless 4D field on T^3_L x R (mode sum, sqrt c = 2 pi) : P TRUE (HZ1 log [1])
  L=8    c(L/2)/c(0)=1.00266e-03  c(L)/c(0)=3.12224e-05  R(L/2)=31.05707
  L=16   c(L/2)/c(0)=1.00266e-03  c(L)/c(0)=3.12224e-05  R(L/2)=31.05707

  ==> the test is satisfied by BOTH P-true and P-false families: it is a consequence
      of w_n >= 0 only.  Per CLAUDE.md 1.1 (a tool must fail on the free field) it is
      VACUOUS as a discriminator: free field / 2D / M6 / HN1 / spread all pass.

====================================================================================================
[4] FIXED-tau BLINDNESS: no fixed-tau correlator decides P  [T]
====================================================================================================
(4a) P FALSE but fixed-tau correlator -> 0:   nu_L = 1/2 delta_{1/L^2} + 1/2 delta_{100}
     L         C(1)         C(L)     P?
     8  4.41248e-01  1.83940e-01  FALSE
    64  4.92248e-01  1.83940e-01  FALSE
   512  4.99024e-01  1.83940e-01  FALSE
  4096  4.99878e-01  1.83940e-01  FALSE

(4b) P TRUE but fixed-tau correlator -> 1:   nu_L = delta_{1/sqrt L}
     8  7.02189e-01  5.91057e-02   TRUE
    64  8.82497e-01  3.35463e-04   TRUE
   512  9.56768e-01  1.48949e-10   TRUE
  4096  9.84496e-01  1.60381e-28   TRUE

  => the two classes share the fixed-tau asymptotics (C(1) -> 0 resp. -> 1 in both).
     The ONLY discriminating scale is tau = L.  Any test built from fixed-tau (or
     scale-covariant) correlator data is blind to P by construction.

